    {
      id: "sirakuza",
      ime: "Syracuse",
      vrsta: "grad",
      sazetak: "Once the most powerful Greek city in the West, today the white Baroque islet of Ortigia surrounded by the sea, and one of the largest ancient theaters.",
      prica: [
        "Syracuse was founded around 734 BC by settlers from Corinth, who first established themselves on the islet of Ortigia. In the 5th century BC it defeated Athens and became the largest city of the Greek world, which Cicero later called the greatest and most beautiful of all Greek cities. Archimedes lived here, and according to tradition he held off the Romans for two years with his war machines, until the city fell in 212 BC and he died by the sword of a Roman soldier.",
        "The apostle Paul stayed in Syracuse for three days on his way to Rome, and Saint Lucy was born and martyred here. The earthquake of 1693 devastated southeastern Sicily, so Ortigia was rebuilt in a pale Baroque stone that turns golden at dusk. The city has been on the UNESCO list since 2005."
      ],
      vidjeti: [
        { ime: "The Cathedral on Ortigia", opis: "The church is literally built into a temple of Athena from the 5th century BC. The space between the Doric columns was simply walled up, so you can still see them emerging from the side wall, outside and in. The building was thus in turn a Greek temple, a Byzantine church, a mosque and a Norman cathedral, and it received its Baroque facade after the earthquake of 1693." },
        { ime: "The Fountain of Arethusa", opis: "Right on the shore of Ortigia, a few meters from the sea, fresh water wells up in a small pool where papyrus grows. Myth says this is the nymph Arethusa, whom the goddess Artemis turned into a spring to save her from the river god Alpheus, and that he flowed from Greece under the sea to be united with her. It was because of this spring that the Greeks settled here in the first place." },
        { ime: "The Greek Theater", opis: "The auditorium, almost one hundred and forty meters in diameter, is carved entirely out of the living rock and held about fifteen thousand people. Aeschylus staged his tragedies here. On the wall of the walkway that divides the seating, look for the carved Greek names of deities and of the ruling family, which were used to mark the sections. Ancient dramas have been performed in it again since 1914." },
        { ime: "The Ear of Dionysius", opis: "Next to the theater is an old quarry that is now an orange grove, and in it an artificial cave twenty-three meters high in the shape of the letter S. It was given its name in 1608 by Caravaggio, recalling the legend that the tyrant Dionysius kept prisoners in it and eavesdropped on their whispers from above. Clap your hands or sing and you will hear why." },
        { ime: "The Temple of Apollo and the market", opis: "At the entrance to Ortigia stand the remains of the oldest stone Doric temple in Sicily, from the 6th century BC. On the topmost step you can still read the inscription with which the builder signed his work, which is a real rarity for a Greek temple. The morning market, with fish, spices and cheeses, begins right behind it." },
        { ime: "Caravaggio's Burial of Saint Lucy", opis: "Fleeing from Malta, Caravaggio painted a large altarpiece for Syracuse in 1608 in which two enormous gravediggers take up the foreground while the body of the saint lies small on the ground. More than half of the canvas is an empty dark wall. The painting spent years in Santa Lucia alla Badia on the Cathedral Square, and since late 2020 it has been back in Santa Lucia al Sepolcro on the mainland, the church it was painted for, though it is still worth checking where it is on display before you go." }
      ],
      jesti: [
        { ime: "Granita con brioche", opis: "The Sicilian breakfast: a creamy iced sorbet of almond, lemon or coffee into which you dip a soft sweet bun with a little ball on top." },
        { ime: "Pasta alla siracusana", opis: "Spaghetti with anchovies, garlic and toasted breadcrumbs, which are known here as the poor man's cheese." },
        { ime: "Tuna and swordfish", opis: "At the market and in the taverns around it you eat fresh grilled tuna and swordfish, and the souvenirs to buy are bottarga, dried tuna roe, and tomatoes from Pachino." },
        { ime: "Nero d'Avola and Moscato di Siracusa", opis: "A red wine from nearby Avola and a sweet muscat considered one of the oldest wines in Italy." }
      ],
      dozivjeti: [
        { ime: "Ancient drama in the Greek Theater", opis: "If you are coming between May and early July, buy a ticket for a performance of a Greek tragedy or comedy at sunset. It is performed in Italian, but the spectacle is worth it even if you do not understand every word.", link: "https://www.indafondazione.org/en/" },
        { ime: "A morning at the Ortigia market", opis: "If you love tasting things, come to the market in the morning, any day except Sunday, and order a platter of cheeses and cured meats or a sandwich assembled in front of you." },
        { ime: "An evening walk around Ortigia", opis: "If you want to watch the stone change color, walk around the islet by the sea at dusk and finish on the Cathedral Square, one of the most beautiful Baroque squares in Italy." },
        { ime: "A day trip to Noto", opis: "If the Baroque appeals to you, half an hour's drive to the south lies Noto, a town that was built anew on a different site after the earthquake of 1693, entirely in honey-colored stone. Look at the balconies of Palazzo Nicolaci, which are held up by stone horses, mermaids and griffins." }
      ],
      agencije: [
        { ime: "Parco archeologico di Siracusa", opis: "The official website of the archaeological park that contains the Greek Theater and the Ear of Dionysius.", link: "https://parchiarcheologici.regione.sicilia.it/siracusa-eloro-villa-tellaro-akrai/" },
        { ime: "Visit Sicily", opis: "The official tourism website of the region of Sicily.", link: "https://www.visitsicily.info/en/" },
        { ime: "Fondazione INDA", opis: "The National Institute of Ancient Drama, which organizes the performances in the Greek Theater.", link: "https://www.indafondazione.org/en/" }
      ],
      kada: "The best times are May and June, when the theater performances are on, and September and October, when the sea is still warm. Stay on Ortigia, and go to the archaeological park early in the morning, before the heat. The feast of Saint Lucy is celebrated on December 13 with a great procession."
    },
    {
      id: "dolomiti",
      ime: "The Dolomites",
      vrsta: "priroda",
      sazetak: "Pale mountains that blush at sunset, with Alpine meadows, three languages and a cuisine where Italy and Austria meet.",
      prica: [
        "About two hundred and fifty million years ago the Dolomites were coral reefs and atolls in a warm sea, so fossil shells are still found in the rocks today. They were named after the French geologist Déodat de Dolomieu, who was the first to describe their distinctive rock in the late 18th century. That rock is why the mountains turn pink at dusk, a phenomenon called enrosadira. A Ladin legend explains it with the enchanted rose garden of the dwarf king Laurin.",
        "The northern part, South Tyrol, belonged to Austria until 1919, so most people here speak German, the signs are bilingual, and in a few valleys Ladin, an old Romance language, is also kept alive. In the First World War the front line between Italy and Austria-Hungary ran across these peaks, and it left behind tunnels, trenches and the iron routes known as via ferrata. The Dolomites have been on the UNESCO list since 2009."
      ],
      vidjeti: [
        { ime: "Tre Cime di Lavaredo", opis: "Three vertical stone teeth are the most recognizable sight in the Dolomites. A loop trail of about ten kilometers with no difficult climbs runs around them, starting at the Auronzo mountain hut, and the toll road up to the hut can now be driven only with a parking reservation made online in advance. Until 1919 the national border ran across the peaks, and the entrances to wartime tunnels can be seen in the rocks beside the trail. The finest view is from the north side, from the Locatelli hut." },
        { ime: "Lago di Braies", opis: "This emerald lake beneath a steep rock face, called Pragser Wildsee in German, can be circled in an hour and a half of easy walking. In the hotel on its shore, in the spring of 1945, a group of prominent prisoners whom the SS had brought from the concentration camps was liberated. From July to mid-September you can drive into the valley during the day only with an online reservation, so book ahead, come early in the morning or take the bus." },
        { ime: "Alpe di Siusi", opis: "The largest high-altitude pasture in Europe stretches over fifty-six square kilometers of rolling meadows with wooden hay barns, above which rise the teeth of the Sassolungo. You get up there by cable car, because the road is closed to traffic during the day. In June and July the meadows are full of flowers." },
        { ime: "Val Gardena and Seceda", opis: "This valley where Ladin is spoken is known for its wood carving, and saints and toys have been carved in Ortisei since the 17th century. A cable car from the village goes up to Seceda, a ridge where the meadow suddenly breaks off into a vertical rock face and the spires of the Odle group look like the teeth of a saw." },
        { ime: "Bolzano and Ötzi the Iceman", opis: "In the capital of South Tyrol, under arcades reminiscent of Austria, there is a museum devoted to the man who died in a glacier about 5,300 years ago and was found there in 1991. You look at him through the little window of a cold chamber, and around him are his clothing, his copper axe and his quiver. He has sixty-one tattoos on his body and, in his shoulder, the tip of the arrow that killed him." },
        { ime: "Cortina d'Ampezzo and Lagazuoi", opis: "This elegant resort ringed by peaks hosted the Winter Olympic Games in 1956 and again in 2026. From the Falzarego Pass a cable car goes up Lagazuoi, a mountain that soldiers in the First World War riddled with tunnels in order to mine each other's positions. You can walk down through the restored tunnels with a helmet and a flashlight." }
      ],
      jesti: [
        { ime: "Canederli", opis: "Dumplings of stale bread, milk and eggs with speck, cheese or spinach, served in broth or with melted butter. In German they are called Knödel." },
        { ime: "Speck Alto Adige", opis: "A pork leg that is first lightly smoked and then dried for months in the mountain air, which places it somewhere between prosciutto and smoked ham. It is cut into thin slices or sticks." },
        { ime: "Casunziei", opis: "Half-moon ravioli from the Cortina area, filled with beets and topped with poppy seeds and melted butter." },
        { ime: "Strudel and Kaiserschmarrn", opis: "Apple strudel and shredded imperial pancake with lingonberry jam are proof that Vienna is close by, and they are at their best in the mountain huts." }
      ],
      dozivjeti: [
        { ime: "Lunch in a mountain hut", opis: "If you want the mountains without the effort, ride up by cable car and have lunch in a hut, which is called a rifugio or a Hütte here. The food is homemade, and the terraces look straight at the rock faces." },
        { ime: "The Reinhold Messner museums", opis: "If the relationship between people and mountains interests you, visit one of the six museums founded by the famous mountaineer, for example the one in Firmian Castle near Bolzano or the one on the summit of Kronplatz.", link: "https://www.messner-mountain-museum.it/en/" },
        { ime: "Törggelen", opis: "If you are coming in October or November, join in the South Tyrolean custom of going from farm to farm to taste the new wine with roasted chestnuts, speck and dumplings." },
        { ime: "The Iceman museum", opis: "If you are short on time but want to see Ötzi, buy your tickets in advance, because the lines are long in summer.", link: "https://www.iceman.it/en" }
      ],
      agencije: [
        { ime: "Südtirol Info", opis: "The official tourism website of South Tyrol.", link: "https://www.suedtirol.info/en/en" },
        { ime: "Dolomiti UNESCO", opis: "The official website of the foundation that looks after the Dolomites as a World Heritage site.", link: "https://www.dolomitiunesco.info/?lang=en" },
        { ime: "Val Gardena", opis: "The valley's official tourism website, with trail maps and cable car timetables.", link: "https://www.valgardena.it/en/" }
      ],
      kada: "Late June, July and September are best for hiking. The cable cars and huts operate roughly from mid-June to early October, and then again in winter for skiing. August is the most crowded month, so set out for the famous spots early in the morning."
    },
    {
      id: "torino",
      ime: "Turin",
      vrsta: "grad",
      sazetak: "The elegant first capital of Italy at the foot of the Alps, a city of arcades, chocolate, vermouth and the oldest Egyptian museum in the world.",
      prica: [
        "The Romans set up the military camp of Augusta Taurinorum here, and its regular plan can still be seen in the straight streets of the center. The city's fate was decided by the House of Savoy, which moved its capital here in 1563 and called in architects to turn it into a Baroque stage for power, with eighteen kilometers of arcades under which the court could stroll even in the rain.",
        "The unification of Italy was directed from Turin, so the city was the country's first capital from 1861 to 1865. When the government moved away, Turin turned to industry: Fiat was founded here in 1899, and Lavazza has its roots here too. Because of the Savoy ties with France it feels a little Parisian, with wide avenues and cafés full of gilding."
      ],
      vidjeti: [
        { ime: "The Egyptian Museum", opis: "Founded in 1824, it is the oldest museum in the world devoted exclusively to Egypt, and Champollion, who deciphered the hieroglyphs, said that the road to Memphis and Thebes passes through Turin. Its most precious treasure is the intact tomb of the architect Kha and his wife Merit, found in 1906 with all its furniture, clothing, food and Merit's wig. In the Gallery of the Kings, statues of pharaohs stand in semidarkness among mirrors." },
        { ime: "The Mole Antonelliana and the Cinema Museum", opis: "The symbol of the city, 167 meters tall, was begun in 1863 as a synagogue, but the architect Antonelli kept adding height until the Jewish community gave up on it, so the city finished it. Today it houses the National Museum of Cinema, where you watch films from reclining chairs, and a glass elevator with no visible shaft rises through the middle of the hall to the viewing platform. The Mole appears on the Italian two-cent coin." },
        { ime: "The Royal Palace and the Chapel of the Holy Shroud", opis: "The Savoy court is connected to the cathedral by a black marble chapel designed in the 17th century by Guarino Guarini. Look up into the dome, built of hexagons that shrink toward the top, which makes it look much taller than it is. The chapel was badly damaged in a fire in 1997 and reopened in 2018. The Shroud of Turin itself is kept in the cathedral and is shown to the public very rarely." },
        { ime: "Piazza San Carlo and the historic cafés", opis: "The square known as the drawing room of Turin is surrounded by arcades that shelter cafés from the 18th and 19th centuries, with chandeliers, mirrors and marble counters. Plans for the unification of Italy were hatched in them, and Count Cavour had his own regular table. At Caffè Mulassano on nearby Piazza Castello, the tramezzino, a triangular sandwich of soft bread, was created in 1926." },
        { ime: "The Basilica of Superga", opis: "On the hill above the city stands a Baroque church that Filippo Juvarra built after the duke made a vow to the Virgin in 1706, should Turin withstand the French siege. In the crypt are the tombs of the Savoy kings. Behind the church is a memorial plaque to the players of the Torino soccer club, whose plane crashed into the wall of the basilica in 1949. An old rack railway runs up to the top." },
        { ime: "Reggia di Venaria", opis: "About ten kilometers from the center, the dukes of Savoy built a hunting palace in the 17th century that is often compared to Versailles. Its finest space is the Great Gallery, a hall eighty meters long with a black and white floor and light pouring in from both sides. The palace had been a barracks and a ruin, and it opened after a major restoration in 2007." }
      ],
      jesti: [
        { ime: "Bicerin", opis: "A hot drink of espresso, hot chocolate and milk foam, served in a glass in three layers that are not stirred together. It has been drunk since the 18th century in the café of the same name opposite the Consolata sanctuary." },
        { ime: "Gianduiotto", opis: "A small chocolate shaped like an upturned boat, made of chocolate and Piedmont hazelnut paste. It was created in the 19th century, when cocoa was expensive, so the chocolatiers of Turin stretched it with hazelnuts, and the same idea later gave rise to Nutella." },
        { ime: "Vitello tonnato and agnolotti del plin", opis: "Cold veal in thin slices with a tuna and caper cream, and tiny ravioli filled with roast meat that are sealed with a pinch, which is what plin means in Piedmontese." },
        { ime: "Vermouth and grissini", opis: "An aromatized wine flavored with wormwood that is drunk here as an aperitif, and thin, crisp breadsticks that, according to tradition, were first baked in the 17th century for a sickly young duke." }
      ],
      dozivjeti: [
        { ime: "The Porta Palazzo market", opis: "If you love markets, this is one of the largest open-air ones in Europe, with a separate section where farmers from the surrounding area sell their own produce. On Saturdays the Balon flea market spreads out next to it as well." },
        { ime: "The Cinema Museum", opis: "If you love the movies, leave yourself at least two hours for the museum in the Mole tower and the elevator to the viewing platform, from which you can see the Alps on a clear day.", link: "https://www.museocinema.it/en" },
        { ime: "An aperitivo under the arcades", opis: "If you want to taste vermouth in the place where it was born, sit down in the early evening in one of the historic cafés around Piazza Castello and order it with ice and orange peel." },
        { ime: "The Automobile Museum", opis: "If the city of Fiat interests you, take a look at the collection of some two hundred vehicles, from the first steam carriages to racing cars.", link: "https://www.museoauto.com/en/" }
      ],
      agencije: [
        { ime: "Turismo Torino e Provincia", opis: "The official tourist board of Turin, with offices, guided tours and a museum card.", link: "https://turismotorino.org/en" },
        { ime: "Museo Egizio", opis: "The official website of the Egyptian Museum, with timed-entry ticket sales.", link: "https://museoegizio.it/en/" },
        { ime: "Musei Reali Torino", opis: "The official website of the Royal Museums, which include the palace, the armory, the gallery and the Chapel of the Holy Shroud.", link: "https://museireali.beniculturali.it/en/" }
      ],
      kada: "Turin is at its loveliest in spring and in fall, when it is white truffle and harvest season in Piedmont. In winter the days are often foggy, but that is when the cafés are at their coziest. Many museums are closed on Mondays or keep shorter hours."
    },
    {
      id: "san-marino",
      ime: "San Marino",
      vrsta: "grad",
      sazetak: "The oldest republic in the world, a tiny state on top of a rock above Rimini that was founded, according to tradition, by a stonemason from the island of Rab.",
      prica: [
        "Tradition says that on September 3, 301, the Christian stonemason Marinus, a native of the island of Rab, fled Diocletian's persecutions and founded a small community on Mount Titano. According to that tradition he had crossed the Adriatic to work on the walls of Rimini and then withdrew to the mountain as a hermit. Legend also attributes to him the last words with which he left his followers free from both masters, which is interpreted as freedom from both the emperor and the pope. The town of Rab and San Marino have been twinned since 1968, and the crossbowmen of Rab are guests at the celebration on September 3.",
        "This little state of sixty-one square kilometers managed to stay independent thanks to its position, its poverty and its deft diplomacy. When Napoleon offered to enlarge its territory in 1797, it politely declined, convinced that it was safer as long as it was small. In 1849 it gave refuge to Garibaldi and his fighters. The state is still headed today by two Captains Regent who are elected every six months, and its statutes of 1600 are counted among the oldest constitutional texts still in force."
      ],
      vidjeti: [
        { ime: "Guaita, the First Tower", opis: "The oldest of the three towers on the ridge of Titano dates from the 11th century and long served as a prison. Drawings and inscriptions made by the prisoners remain on the cell walls. It stands on the very edge of the cliff, and on a clear day you can see the Adriatic coast from Rimini southward from the ramparts." },
        { ime: "Cesta, the Second Tower", opis: "It was built on the highest point of the mountain, at about 750 meters, and it holds a museum of historic weapons with suits of armor, crossbows and halberds. The path between the first and second towers, called the Witches' Pass, is the loveliest walk in the country. The third tower, Montale, can be seen from the path, but you cannot go inside." },
        { ime: "Palazzo Pubblico and Piazza della Libertà", opis: "The town hall in the Gothic Revival style was completed in 1894 and is the seat of the parliament and the Captains Regent. On the facade, look for the coats of arms of four of the republic's settlements and the mosaic with Saint Marinus. In summer the guard, in green coats and red trousers, is changed in front of the palace several times a day." },
        { ime: "The Basilica of Saint Marinus", opis: "This 19th-century Neoclassical church stands on the site of the old parish church and keeps the saint's relics under the high altar. Beside it is the little church of Saint Peter, in whose rock two niches are carved that tradition says were the beds of Saint Marinus and his companion Saint Leo." },
        { ime: "The State Museum", opis: "Palazzo Pergami Belluzzi displays archaeological finds from Titano, paintings and objects connected with the history of the republic. One interesting section holds the gifts sent to San Marino in the 19th century by distinguished people from all over Europe when the small state decided to found a museum." }
      ],
      jesti: [
        { ime: "Torta Tre Monti", opis: "A round cake of thin wafer layers with hazelnut and chocolate cream, edged with chocolate and named after the three towers. It has been made since 1942 and is the most common edible souvenir." },
        { ime: "Piadina", opis: "A thin flatbread cooked on a griddle and filled with prosciutto, soft squacquerone cheese and arugula, the same as in neighboring Romagna." },
        { ime: "Bustrengo", opis: "An old peasant cake of cornmeal, breadcrumbs, apples, figs and honey, which was baked for the holidays." },
        { ime: "Local wines", opis: "Sangiovese and muscat are grown on the slopes of Titano, and wines bearing the state mark of origin are sold by the consortium of San Marino winemakers." }
      ],
      dozivjeti: [
        { ime: "By cable car from Borgo Maggiore", opis: "If you want the most beautiful arrival, park in Borgo Maggiore at the foot of the mountain and ride up on the cable car, which reaches the old town in two minutes, with a view of the sea." },
        { ime: "The celebration on September 3", opis: "If you are coming in early September, on the anniversary of the republic's founding you will see a parade in historical uniforms, a crossbow competition and fireworks." },
        { ime: "The investiture of the Captains Regent", opis: "If you happen to be here on April 1 or October 1, you will watch the ceremonial procession in which the two new heads of state take office, following a ceremonial that has not changed much in centuries." },
        { ime: "A stamp in your passport", opis: "If you like keepsakes, you can get a San Marino tourist stamp in your passport at the tourist office for a fee, even though there is no control of any kind at the border." }
      ],
      agencije: [
        { ime: "Visit San Marino", opis: "The official website of the State Tourism Office, with a range of guided tours.", link: "https://www.visitsanmarino.com/pub2/VisitSM/en/" },
        { ime: "Istituti Culturali di San Marino", opis: "The State Museums of San Marino, which run the towers, the State Museum and Palazzo Pubblico.", link: "https://www.museidistato.sm/" }
      ],
      kada: "It is most pleasant from May to September, and one day is enough for a visit. A direct bus runs from Rimini and takes about fifty minutes to reach the old town. Come in the morning or stay until dusk, when the day-trippers leave."
    }
